Let $\alpha = \arcsin 0.4$ and $\beta = \arcsin 0.5$. Then $\sin \alpha = 0.4$ and $\sin \beta = 0.5$. We want to find $\sin (\alpha + \beta) \cdot \sin (\beta - \alpha)$.

Using the angle addition and subtraction formulas, we have
\begin{align*}
\sin (\alpha + \beta) &= \sin \alpha \cos \beta + \cos \alpha \sin \beta, \\
\sin (\beta - \alpha) &= \sin \beta \cos \alpha - \cos \beta \sin \alpha.
\end{align*}

Substituting the values of $\sin \alpha$, $\sin \beta$, and $\cos \alpha = \sqrt{1 - \sin^2 \alpha} = \sqrt{1 - 0.4^2} = \sqrt{0.84}$, we get
\begin{align*}
\sin (\alpha + \beta) &= 0.4 \cdot \sqrt{0.84} + \sqrt{0.84} \cdot 0.5, \\
\sin (\beta - \alpha) &= 0.5 \cdot \sqrt{0.84} - \sqrt{0.84} \cdot 0.4.
\end{align*}

Multiplying these two expressions, we get
\begin{align*}
\sin (\alpha + \beta) \cdot \sin (\beta - \alpha) &= (0.4 \cdot \sqrt{0.84} + \sqrt{0.84} \cdot 0.5)(0.5 \cdot \sqrt{0.84} - \sqrt{0.84} \cdot 0.4) \\
&= (0.4 \cdot \sqrt{0.84} + 0.5 \cdot \sqrt{0.84})(0.5 \cdot \sqrt{0.84} - 0.4 \cdot \sqrt{0.84}) \\
&= (0.4 + 0.5)(0.5 - 0.4) \cdot 0.84 \\
&= 0.9 \cdot 0.1 \cdot 0.84 \\
&= \boxed{0.0756}.
\end{align*}

The answer is: 0.0756

