The equation of the ellipse can be rewritten as $\frac{x^2}{\frac{1}{k}} + y^2 = 1.$  This is an ellipse with semi-major axis $\sqrt{\frac{1}{k}}$ and semi-minor axis $1.$  The distance between the foci is $2c,$ where $c = \sqrt{a^2 - b^2} = \sqrt{\frac{1}{k} - 1}.$  The foci are located at $(\pm c, 0).$

[asy]
unitsize(2 cm);

real k = 2;
pair F1, F2, P, Q;

F1 = (-sqrt(1/k - 1),0);
F2 = (sqrt(1/k - 1),0);
P = (sqrt(1/k),0);
Q = (sqrt(1/k - 1),sqrt(1/k - 1));

draw(yscale(1)*yscale(1)*xscale(sqrt(1/k))*yscale(sqrt(1/k))*Circle((0,0),1));
draw(yscale(1)*yscale(1)*xscale(sqrt(1/k))*yscale(sqrt(1/k))*Circle((0,0),sqrt(1/k - 1)));
draw(F1--F2);
draw(P--Q);

dot("$F_1$", F1, S);
dot("$F_2$", F2, S);
dot("$P$", P, S);
dot("$Q$", Q, NE);
[/asy]

The circle is tangent to the ellipse at $P = (\sqrt{\frac{1}{k}}, 0)$ and $Q = (\sqrt{\frac{1}{k} - 1}, \sqrt{\frac{1}{k} - 1}).$  Since $P$ lies on the $x$-axis, the center of the circle must lie on the $y$-axis.  Let the center of the circle be $(0, r).$  Then the radius of the circle is $r,$ and the equation of the circle is $x^2 + (y - r)^2 = r^2.$

Since $Q$ lies on the circle, we have
\[(\sqrt{\frac{1}{k} - 1})^2 + (\sqrt{\frac{1}{k} - 1} - r)^2 = r^2.\]This simplifies to
\[\frac{1}{k} - 1 + \frac{1}{k} - 1 - 2r \sqrt{\frac{1}{k} - 1} + r^2 = r^2.\]This becomes
\[2 \left( \frac{1}{k} - 1 \right) = 2r \sqrt{\frac{1}{k} - 1}.\]Then $r = \frac{1}{k} - 1.$  The distance between the foci is $2c = 2 \sqrt{\frac{1}{k} - 1},$ so the radius of the circle is half the distance between the foci.  Therefore,
\[\frac{1}{k} - 1 = \sqrt{\frac{1}{k} - 1}.\]Squaring both sides, we get
\[\frac{1}{k^2} - \frac{2}{k} + 1 = \frac{1}{k} - 1.\]This simplifies to
\[\frac{1}{k^2} - \frac{3}{k} + 2 = 0.\]Multiplying both sides by $k^2,$ we get
\[1 - 3k + 2k^2 = 0.\]This factors as $(k - 1)(2k - 1) = 0,$ so $k = 1$ or $k = \frac{1}{2}.$  Since $k > 1,$ we must have $k = \boxed{\frac{1}{2}}.$

